<html lang="en" op="item"><head><meta name="referrer" content="origin"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"><link rel="icon" href="y18.svg"><link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="news.css?YWKanJBx0mFYilvdBP0Q"><link rel="canonical" href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50304674"><title>&gt; Alphabet reported property-and-equipment purchases of about $51.4B, while anal... | Hacker News</title></head><body><center><table id="hnmain" border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="85%" bgcolor="#f6f6ef"><tr><td bgcolor="#ff6600"><table border="0" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" width="100%" style="padding:2px"><tr><td style="width:18px;padding-right:4px"><a href="https://news.ycombinator.com"><img src="y18.svg" width="18" height="18" style="border:1px white solid; display:block"></a></td><td style="line-height:12pt; height:10px;"><span class="pagetop"><b class="hnname"><a href="news">Hacker News</a></b><a href="newest">new</a> | <a href="front">past</a> | <a href="newcomments">comments</a> | <a href="ask">ask</a> | <a href="show">show</a> | <a href="jobs">jobs</a> | <a href="submit" rel="nofollow">submit</a></span></td><td style="text-align:right;padding-right:4px;"><span class="pagetop"><a href="login?goto=item%3Fid%3D50304674">login</a></span></td></tr></table></td></tr><tr style='height:10px'/><tr id="bigbox"><td><table class="fatitem" border="0"><tr class="athing" id="50304674"><td class="ind"></td><td valign="top" class="votelinks"><center><a id='up_50304674' href='vote?id=50304674&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50304674'><div class='votearrow' title='upvote'></div></a></center></td><td class="default"><div style="margin-top:2px; margin-bottom:-10px;"><span class="comhead"><a href="user?id=_aavaa_" class="hnuser">_aavaa_</a> <span class="age" title="2026-10-30T14:43:41"><a href="item?id=50304674">1 day ago</a></span> <span id="unv_50304674"></span><span class="navs"> | <a href="item?id=50303833">parent</a> | <a href="item?id=50303833#50304674" rel="nofollow">context</a> | <a href="fave?id=50304674">favorite</a><span class="onstory"> |  on: <a href="item?id=50303833" title="AI Bubble Burst">AI Bubble Burst</a></span></span></span></div><br>
<div class="comment"><div class="commtext c00">&gt; Alphabet reported property-and-equipment purchases of about $51.4B, while analysts expected approximately $55.1B. Microsoft reported capex and finance leases of about $45.2B, while analysts were expecting around $50.7B. These were significant misses.<p>&gt;<p>&gt; […]<p>&gt;<p>&gt; Caterpillar inventories increased to $22.2 billion at the end of September […] The 22% increase in inventory signals future demand weakness or overproduction, and the reason is lower AI capex, as reported by Alphabet and Microsoft.<p>Maybe I’m not getting it. But it’s alphabet and Microsoft spending less than <i>someone external</i> said they should spend, not what they themselves said they would spend. How is that a miss?</div><div class="reply"></div></div></td></tr><tr style='height:6px'/><tr><td colspan="2"></td><td><form action="comment" method="post"><input type="hidden" name="parent" value="50304674"><input type="hidden" name="goto" value="item?id=50304674"><input type="hidden" name="hmac" value="9afc0aae460783a30e1764e9b34ab4fa6f5c34b4"><textarea name="text" rows="8" cols="80" wrap="virtual" style="vertical-align:bottom"></textarea>&nbsp;<a href="formatdoc" tabindex="-1"><font size="-2" color="#AFAFAF">help</font></a><br><br>
<input type="submit" value="reply"></form></td></tr></table><br>
<table border="0" class="comment-tree"><tr class="athing comtr" id="50304885"><td><table border="0"><tr><td class="ind" indent="0"><img src="s.gif" height="1" width="0"></td><td valign="top" class="votelinks"><center><a id='up_50304885' href='vote?id=50304885&amp;how=up&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50304674'><div class='votearrow' title='upvote'></div></a></center></td><td class="default"><div style="margin-top:2px; margin-bottom:-10px;"><span class="comhead"><a href="user?id=goodmythical" class="hnuser">goodmythical</a> <span class="age" title="2026-10-30T15:01:06"><a href="item?id=50304885">1 day ago</a></span> <span id="unv_50304885"></span><span class="navs"> <a class="togg clicky" id="50304885" n="1" href="javascript:void(0)">[–]</a><span class="onstory"></span></span></span></div><br>
<div class="comment"><div class="commtext c00">Lots of investors &quot;buy the news&quot; meaning that they go based on under&#x2F;over speculator sentiment.<p>If it was believed that they be purchasing N, then that belief was priced in. If they only bought N-1, the price needs to be adjusted to reflect the new information.<p>Here, the argument is &quot;the more hardware we have, the more compute we can sell, the more compute we sell, the more profit we&#x27;ll have&quot; and so investors <i>were</i> expecting a higher level of profit and now are not.<p>All of this is why stock value and company value often share little with each other. The people working at the company and the people investing in the company have different views of what one small piece of the company is worth. You&#x27;ll see PE ratios vary by sector, over time, by strength of underlying economy, near lows&#x2F;highs etc. Any company&#x27;s value is only set by what people are willing to pay for it at any given time.</div><div class="reply"><p><font size="1"><u><a href="reply?id=50304885&amp;goto=item%3Fid%3D50304674%2350304885" rel="nofollow">reply</a></u></font></div></div></td></tr></table></td></tr></table><br><br>
</td></tr><tr><td><img src="s.gif" height="10" width="0"><table width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="1"><tr><td bgcolor="#ff6600"></td></tr></table><br>
